  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to use a hybrid optimization approach combining Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO) for global exploration with local refinement via L-BFGS-B. This addresses the local optimum bottleneck by leveraging population diversity from PSO while using precise gradient-based methods to improve promising candidates. The particle swarm maintains a balance between exploration (via inertia weight) and exploitation (via personal/global best guidance), while the L-BFGS-B step ensures local convergence. This differs from the previous approach by eliminating random perturbations and instead using structured swarm dynamics for exploration.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nRepresentation: A step function h(x) with n_points samples over [0, 2], where 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and \u2211h[i] = n_points / 2. The objective function is:\n\nC\u2085 = max_k [\u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1 - h(x+k)) dx] * dx\n\nConstraints: \n- \u2211h[i] = n_points / 2 (equality constraint)\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 (bound constraints)\n\nImplementation: Use PSO with:\n- Particle positions as vectors of h values\n- Objective as C\u2085\n- Inertia weight (0.5-0.9) decaying linearly\n- Social cognitive coefficients (c1=2.0, c2=2.0)\n- Mutation amplitude 0.02-0.05 (adaptive per generation)\n\nLocal refinement via L-BFGS-B with:\n- Constraints implemented as a penalty function\n- Gradient evaluation via finite differences\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na) Input: n_points = 400, initial_h_values (if available)\nb) Initialize swarm:\n   - If initial_h_values exists:\n      i) Reshape to (n_points,)\n      ii) Normalize to \u2211h = n_points / 2\n      iii) Convert to particle positions\n   - Else:\n      i) Randomly sample n_particles = 40 positions\n      ii) For each particle:\n         - Generate random starting h as uniform distribution\n         - Normalize to \u2211h = n_points / 2\n         - Apply low-pass filter to reduce kurtosis\n\nc) PSO loop (max 200 generations):\n   i) For each particle:\n      - Evaluate C\u2085 via np.correlate with full mode\n      - Compute fitness = -C\u2085 (minimization)\n      - Update personal best (pbest) if fitness improved\n   ii) Calculate global best (gbest) from all pbest\n   iii) Update particle velocities:\n         v = \u03c9*v + c1*r1*(pbest - pos) + c2*r2*(gbest - pos)\n         (\u03c9 decays from 0.9 to 0.4 over generations)\n   iv) Update particle positions\n   v) Clip values to [0, 1] and rescale to maintain \u2211h = n_points / 2\n   vi) Apply adaptive mutation: \n         for each dimension, \n         new_val = pos[i] + mutation_amp * np.random.normal(0, 1)\n         (mutation_amp decays from 0.05 to 0.02)\n\nd) Track best solution:\n   - Maintain top 5% of particles as elites\n   - Evaluate all elite candidates every 5 generations\n\ne) Final refinement:\n   - Select top 5 elite particles\n   - For each, apply L-BFGS-B optimization with:\n      * Objective function: -C\u2085\n      * Constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n      * Bounds: (0.0, 1.0) for all dimensions\n      * Max iterations: 50\n      * Tolerance: 1e-8\n   - Choose the particle with lowest final C\u2085\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault configuration:\n- n_particles = 40\n- Mutation amplitude = 0.05 \u2192 0.02 over 200 generations\n- Inertia weight = 0.9 \u2192 0.4\n- Social cognitive coefficients = c1 = c2 = 2.0\n- Elite fraction = 5%\n- Refinement steps = 50\n\nVariations:\n- Initial distribution: Use truncated normal instead of uniform\n- Annealing schedule: Stochastic gradient descent with learning rate decay\n- Population diversity: Randomized restarts every 50 generations\n- Initialization: Use low-pass filtered versions of previous best\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTime allocation:\n- 70% for PSO exploration (140s)\n- 20% for L-BFGS refinement (40s)\n- 10% for constraint handling and mutation (20s)\n\nTermination checks:\n- After 200 generations (600s)\n- If best C\u2085 stagnates for 30 generations\n- If max iter reached\n\nFallback:\n- If parent exists, use it as baseline\n- If no valid candidate, return parent with penalty term 0.01\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nChecks:\n- All h[i] \u2208 [0,1] (via clipping)\n- \u2211h = n_points / 2 (via rescaling)\n- Objective evaluation matches np.correlate with full mode\n\nRe-evaluation:\n- Final candidate re-evaluated with exact evaluator\n- Sanity check: max lag overlap \u2264 0.38085\n- Validate feasibility with sum check\n- Confirm bounds using np.all((h >= 0) & (h <= 1))",
